speechtext:
I am not quite that old;
I am living in the year 1945. If the hon. member for Cape Breton South wants to go back and live in the days of seventy-five years ago, he is welcome to do so. I deplore the fact that a member of this house should have made the statement that fell from the lips of the hon. member for Cape Breton South this afternoon concerning members of the Ontario government. They have nothing to learn about being fair to labour or looking after the rights of labour; they have nothing to learn about-trying to bring about a happy and amicable settlement of this difficult strike.
It was no contribution to the facts that was made by the hon. member for Cape Breton South this afternoon; it was a contribution to prejudice; it was an inflammatory contribution. I say that that inflammatory contribution, lacking as it did reference to the facts of the situation in Ontario, contained false accusations against the Premier of Ontario and the attorney-general. I hope that a responsible attitude will be taken by members of the house in their approach to this question. The very least that can be asked of hon. members is that they do not fan the flames of feeling which exist to-day in the city of Windsor. If we have not anything else to contribute, let us at least refrain from fanning those flames.
